\ Type I (sequential) Wald tests in a mixed model: Genstat's hypotheses.
\ Genstat counterpart of dev/sas/type1-exam8.2.sas, for predictmeans
\ anova(type = "I", type1_basis = "fitted", ddf = "KR-average") (R/anova.R).
\ Please save the whole Output window (File > Save) and send that file; the
\ first run may need small syntax fixes.
\
\ Data: Example 8.2 of Williams, Harwood and Matheson (2023), Eucalyptus
\ clone trial (eda4treeR::DataExam8.2): 5 replicates x 10 rows x 6 columns,
\ 56 test clones and 4 standards. Columns: repl row column contcompf
\ (1 = test clone, 0 = standard) standard (1 = none/test clone, 2 = PN14,
\ 3 = SSOseed, 4 = U6, 5 = UG323) clone (code 1 = "0" for the standards,
\ 2-57 the UP clones in alphabetical order) contcompv (variate, = contcompf)
\ dbh.
\
\ Model (as the SAS program): fixed repl + column + contcompf +
\ contcompf.standard; random repl.row, repl.column and contcompv.clone (clone
\ effects for test clones only), REML.
\
\ R values (lme4 REML) to compare:
\   variance components: repl.row 0.08023, repl.column 0.05284,
\   contcompv.clone 0.4950, residual 0.3992
\   Run 1, fixed repl + column + contcompf + contcompf.standard
\                       sequential             dropping
\     term              Wald    d.d.f.          Wald   d.d.f.
\     repl               8.20    26.64           8.20   26.64
\     column             7.99    19.43           7.77   19.72
\     contcompf         72.66   254.01            -       -
\     contcompf.std     51.75   208.20          51.75  208.20
\   Run 2, fixed contcompf + repl + column + contcompf.standard
\     contcompf 70.34 (254.64), repl 8.20 (26.64), column 10.32 (19.46),
\     contcompf.standard 51.75 (208.20)
\   (SAS HTYPE=1 builds the sequential hypotheses from X'X instead: run 2
\   column F 2.00 = Wald 10.0 with KR, contcompf 68.68.)
\   Means of contcompf.standard: standards PN14 7.703 (s.e. 0.3146),
\   SSOseed 6.084 (0.3125), U6 6.554 (0.3134), UG323 8.971 (0.3137); test
\   clones 8.908 (0.1168).
\ CADJUST=none: contcompv must not be centred. Centred (Genstat's default,
\ run of 2026-11-25, genstat-results-2026-11-25-type1-centred.txt) it is
\ -0.933 for the standards, which then share one random clone effect,
\ confounded with contcompf: a different model (contcompv.clone 111.4,
\ contcompf Wald 0.03 on 38.7 d.d.f.).
\ Questions: (1) does Genstat print the drop-term d.d.f. (19.7) for column
\ in the sequential table of run 1, where the sequential hypothesis gives
\ 19.4? (2) run 2 checks the sequential definition with a different order.

UNITS [NVALUES=300]
FACTOR [LEVELS=5] repl
FACTOR [LEVELS=10] row
FACTOR [LEVELS=6] column
FACTOR [LEVELS=!(0, 1)] contcompf
FACTOR [LEVELS=5; LABELS=!t('0', PN14, SSOseed, U6, UG323)] standard
FACTOR [LEVELS=57] clone
VARIATE contcompv, dbh
READ repl, row, column, contcompf, standard, clone, contcompv, dbh
